This is the most nit-picky of nit-picks, since it doesn't really matter here, but `Date.now()` shouldn't be used for calculating stuff like this.

JavaScript has a [`performance.now()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Performance/now) function instead (see the MDN docs on why `Date.now()` is bad).

Buuuut, we could even use [`console.time()` and `console.timeEnd()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/console/time) instead:

```suggestion
  console.time("Rebuilt");
  await Promise.all(contexts.map((ctx) => ctx.rebuild()));
  console.timeEnd("Rebuilt");
```

This will print something that looks like `Rebuilt: 2763.864013671875 ms`.

The only issue is that using `console.time()`/`console.timeEnd()` won't work if `rebuildAll()` is being called multiple times at the same time (is that an issue?).
